WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.867
OpenCV.

2
00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:08.333
No learning involved, just fast, battle tested image operations that wrap around every deep model.

3
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:15.167
OpenCV, imported as cv2, is the workhorse of classical image processing.

4
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:17.000
Resize, crop and warp.

5
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.600
Thresholding, which splits pixels above and below a value.

6
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.167
Canny edge detection.

7
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.739
And contours, the outlines of connected regions.

8
00:00:25.739 --> 00:00:28.533
Images are plain NumPy arrays throughout.

9
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:31.167
Here's a typical chain.

10
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:32.800
Convert to grayscale.

11
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.433
Blur to remove noise.

12
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.800
Run Canny to make edges pop out.

13
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.267
Find the contours.

14
00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:40.576
And keep only the large ones.

15
00:00:40.576 --> 00:00:46.467
Sometimes this replaces a model entirely: bright blobs on a dark sky need no network.

16
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:51.268
Now the gotcha worth tattooing somewhere.

17
00:00:51.268 --> 00:00:54.167
cv2 loads images as BGR, not RGB.

18
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.088
Feed that to a model and predictions turn to garbage.

19
00:00:58.088 --> 00:00:59.865
It's the number one cause.

20
00:00:59.865 --> 00:01:01.300
Convert to RGB first.

21
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.700
Here it is in code.

22
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.233
imread returns a NumPy array in BGR order.

23
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.700
Then gray, blur, and Canny edges.

24
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.137
Find contours and keep those with area above five hundred.

25
00:01:14.137 --> 00:01:17.133
No model, no GPU, about five milliseconds.

26
00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:20.300
Here's OpenCV's real role.

27
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:26.000
Every satellite tile passes through a cv2 gauntlet before any deep model sees it.

28
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:32.700
Convert BGR to RGB, the bug that once cost a full day, and resize to two twenty four.

29
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.033
A cheap blur check then discards unusable frames in five milliseconds, instead of wasting GPU time.

30
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:42.147
Let's recap.

31
00:01:42.147 --> 00:01:46.333
OpenCV gives fast classical operations on NumPy arrays.

32
00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:49.967
It's the pre and post processing layer around deep models.

33
00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:53.467
Sometimes threshold and contours beat training a network.

34
00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:58.167
And always remember: cv2 loads BGR, not RGB.
